#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/02-conditions-and-loops.md:145
msgid "Different types"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/02-conditions-and-loops.md:147
msgid "Python does not compare \"apples with oranges\". `<`, `>`, `<=`, `>=` between a number and a string are an error:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/02-conditions-and-loops.md:154
msgid "`==` does not give an error - it is simply `False`. This is a real trap with data from files, where every value is a string:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/02-conditions-and-loops.md:163
msgid "Numbers of different types compare as you expect: `1 == 1.0` is `True`, and `True == 1` is `True` too."
msgstr ""

#. box title
#: site/lectures/02-conditions-and-loops.md:166
msgid "Best practice"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/02-conditions-and-loops.md:167
msgid "Make sure you compare values of the **same type**. Convert the data first - `int()`, `float()`, `str()` (session 1) - and then compare."
msgstr ""

#. box title
#: site/lectures/02-conditions-and-loops.md:177
msgid "Python 2 was different"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/02-conditions-and-loops.md:178
msgid "Python 2 could compare values of different types: `\"2\" >= 2` was `True`, `99999 >= \"2\"` was `False` - without any error, and without any sense. If you meet old code that does this, it is a bug in Python 3."
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/02-conditions-and-loops.md:183
msgid "Strings are compared letter by letter"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/02-conditions-and-loops.md:185
msgid "Strings are compared **lexicographically**, like words in a dictionary: the first characters first; if they are equal, the second ones, and so on. Each character is compared by its number (its *code point*) in the Unicode table, and [`ord()`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#ord) shows that number:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/02-conditions-and-loops.md:197
msgid "`\"9\" < \"1000\"` is `False`, because only the first characters decide: `\"9\"` (57) is not less than `\"1\"` (49). Numbers stored as text sort wrongly - convert them first."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/02-conditions-and-loops.md:200
msgid "Dates in the ISO format `YYYY-MM-DD` (the format of the sample data) are an exception that works: the year comes first, then the month, then the day, all with a fixed number of digits. In the Bulgarian format `DD.MM.YYYY` they do not:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/02-conditions-and-loops.md:209
msgid "Real dates (`datetime`) come in session 7."
msgstr ""

