#. heading 2
#: site/lectures/02-conditions-and-loops.md:818
msgid "9. Operator precedence"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/02-conditions-and-loops.md:820
msgid "The order in which the parts of an expression are calculated decides the result. Wrong assumptions about it lead to bugs:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/02-conditions-and-loops.md:828
msgid "The short version of the table (the full one in session 1, section 11, and in the [Python reference](https://docs.python.org/3/reference/expressions.html#operator-precedence)): arithmetic, then comparisons (`<`, `==`, `in`, `is`, ...), then `not`, then `and`, then `or`, and the conditional expression `... if ... else ...` last of all."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/02-conditions-and-loops.md:833
msgid "**Use brackets for clarity.** Even when you know the rules, brackets make the expression easier to read for others."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/02-conditions-and-loops.md:835
msgid "**Avoid complicated expressions.** If an expression grows too long, split it: give the parts names."
msgstr ""

#. heading 2
#: site/lectures/02-conditions-and-loops.md:850
msgid "10. Loops"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/02-conditions-and-loops.md:852
msgid "A **loop** runs a block of code **many times**. Each run of the block is an **iteration**. Python has two loops:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/02-conditions-and-loops.md:855
msgid "`while` repeats **while a condition is true** - when you do not know in advance how many times (section 11);"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/02-conditions-and-loops.md:857
msgid "`for` repeats **for each item** of a sequence - the rows of a file, the items of a list, the numbers of a `range` (section 12)."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/02-conditions-and-loops.md:860
msgid "You already use loops in SAS: the **DATA step is a loop** - it runs once for each row of the input data set, without you writing it. In Python you write the loop yourself (and in pandas, again, you do not - session 14)."
